==Old English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|ang|sċeolan}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|ang|gmw-pro|ine-pro|inh=1}}
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*skulaną||to owe}}. Cognate with {{cog|ofs|skela}}, {{cog|osx|skulan}}, {{cog|goh|sculan}}, {{cog|non|skulu}}, {{cog|got|𐍃𐌺𐌿𐌻𐌰𐌽}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{ang-IPA|sċulan}}

===Verb===
{{ang-verb|sċulan}}

# {{lb|ang|auxiliary}} [[should]]
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [https://books.google.com/books?id=m2hVAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA390 "Likewise of Saint Peter"]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Wē strange '''sċulon''' beran þāra unstrangena byrðenne.''|Strong people like us '''should''' bear the burden of the weak.}}
#* '''c. 992''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], [http://www.oldenglishaerobics.net/aelfric_job.html "The First Sunday in September"]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ġif wē gōd underfēngon of Godes handa, hwȳ ne '''sċulon''' wē ēac yfel underfōn?''|If we've accepted good things from God's hand, why '''should'''n't we accept bad things too?}}
#* ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=ym1QAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA38 The Legend of St. Andrew]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ġēa hlāford, and hwæt ġif iċ swelcne mann ġemēte? Hwelċe mēde '''sċeal''' iċ him behātan?''|Yes lord, and what if I find someone like that? What kind of reward '''should''' I promise them?}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA123 translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ne '''sċeal''' nān mann sēocne mannan and ġesārgodne swenċan, ac hine man '''sċeal''' lǣdan tō þām lǣċe þæt hē his tiliġe.''|No one '''should''' harass a miserably sick person, but they '''should''' be taken to the doctor so they can be treated.}}
#* '''late 10th century''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[[w:Old English Hexateuch|the Old English Hexateuch]]'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=QrZUAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA202 Deuteronomy 1:22]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Uton sendan sċēaweras þæt sċēawiġen þæt land and cȳðen ūs on hwelcne weġ wē faran '''sċulon''' and tō hwelcum burgum.''|Let's send spies who can survey the land and tell us which way we '''should''' go and to what cities.}}
# {{lb|ang|auxiliary}} [[must]]
#* ''{{w|Beowulf}}'', [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Beowulf_(Harrison_and_Sharp) line 455]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Gǣþ ā wyrd swā hēo '''sċeal'''.''|Fate goes always as it '''must'''.}}
#* '''early 11th century''', anonymous {{w|gloss}} of [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric's]] {{w|Latin}} [https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/%C3%86lfric%27s_Colloquy Colloquy] (c. 995)
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ǣlċe dæġe iċ '''sċeal''' erian fulne æcer oþþe mā.''|Every day I '''have to''' plow a full acre or more.}}
#* '''c. 897''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=6JEtAAAAYAAJ&pg=PA276 translation] of [[w:Pope Gregory I|Pope Gregory's]] ''{{w|Pastoral Care}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Sēo burg þæs mōdes '''sċeal''' swīðe oft ġefrēdan hire fēonda speru.''|The fortress of the mind '''must''' very often feel the spears of its enemies.}}
#* '''c. 995''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric]], ''[https://archive.org/stream/grammatik00aelfuoft#page/n209/mode/2up Extracts on Grammar in English]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þæt word willan næfþ nān bebēodendlīċ, for þon þe sē willa '''sċeal''' bēon ǣfre frī.''|The word "to want" has no {{glossary|imperative mood|imperative}}, because the will '''must''' always be free.}}
#* ''{{w|Anglo-Saxon Chronicle}}'', [https://books.google.com/books?id=p8CD9UyZnWMC&pg=PA23 Manuscript E], [https://books.google.com/books?id=FvlsBQAAQBAJ&pg=PA360 year 605]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Augustīnus cwæþ, "Ġif Wēalas nyllaþ sibbe wiþ ūs, hīe '''sċulon''' æt Seaxena handa forweorðan."''|Augustine said, "If the Celts don't want peace with us, they'll '''have to''' perish at the hands [lit. "hand"] of the Saxons."}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=gIUnAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA43 translation] of [[w:Augustine of Hippo|Saint Augustine's]] ''[[w:Soliloquies of Augustine|Soliloquies]]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Hū, ne sæġde iċ ǣr þæt sē þe bær līċ ġefrēdan wolde, þæt hē hit '''sċolde''' mid barum handum ġefrēdan?''|Didn't I say before that if you want to feel someone's bare body, you '''have to''' feel it with your bare hands?}}
#* '''c. 996''', [[w:Ælfric of Eynsham|Ælfric's]] ''[https://books.google.com/books?id=jLYLAQAAIAAJ&pg=PA522 Lives of Saints]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þā behēoldon swīðe ġeorne þā ċeapmenn hine, and be him on ġeþance smēadon hwæt manna hē bēon '''sċolde'''.''|The businessmen looked at him very closely and tried to figure out what kind of person he '''must''' be.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=xtM0rOEXqJIC&pg=PA52 translation] of [[w:Orosius|Orosius’]] ''History Against the Pagans''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Ne wēne iċ, nū iċ lang spell hæbbe tō seċġenne, þæt iċ hīe on þisse bēċ ġeendian mæġe, ac iċ ōðre onġinnan '''sċeal'''.''|Since I have some long stories to tell, I don't think I can finish them in this book, so I'll '''have to''' start another one.}}
# to [[be supposed to]] do something (used like "supposedly" or "they say...")
#* '''late 9th century''', [https://books.google.com/books?id=YD9HAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA148 translation] of [[w:Bede|Bede's]] ''[[w:Ecclesiastical History of the English People|Ecclesiastical History]]''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þēah þe hē cristen bēon '''sċolde''', nolde hē nāne āre witan þǣre cristenan ǣfæstnesse.''|Though he was '''supposedly''' a Christian, he refused to show any respect for the Christian religion.}}
#* '''late 9th century''', [[w:Alfred the Great|King Alfred's]] [https://books.google.com/books?id=8sy8OOmW-ZAC&pg=PA98 translation] of [[w:Boethius|Boethius']] ''{{w|The Consolation of Philosophy}}''
#*: {{quote|ang|''Iċ wāt þæt þū ġehīerdest oft reċċan on ealdum lēasum spellum þætte Iob Sāturnes sunu '''sċolde''' bēon þæt hīehste god ofer eall ōðru godu, and hē '''sċolde''' bēon þæs heofones sunu and '''sċolde''' rīcsian on heofonum, and '''sċolden''' gigantas bēon eorðan suna and þā '''sċolden''' rīcsian ofer eorðan, and þā '''sċolden''' hīe bēon swelċe hīe wǣron ġesweostrenu bearn, for þon þe hē '''sċolde''' bēon heofones sunu and hīe eorðan, and þā '''sċolde''' þām gigantum ofþynċan þæt hē hæfde heora rīċe, wolden þā tōbrecan þone heofon under him. Þā '''sċolde''' hē sendan þunras and līeġete and windas, and tōweorpan eall heora ġeweorc mid, and hīe selfe ofslēan.''|I know you've heard a lot of made-up old stories about how Jupiter, Saturn's son, was '''supposedly''' the supreme god over all other gods, and he was '''supposedly''' the son of the sky and '''supposedly''' ruled in the heavens, and giants '''supposedly''' were the sons of the Earth and '''supposedly''' ruled over Earth, and they were '''supposedly''' like cousins because he was '''supposed to be''' from the sky while the giants were from the Earth. Then the giants '''supposedly''' got jealous that he had ''their'' kingdom and wanted to destroy the sky from under him. Then he '''supposedly''' sent thunder and lightning and winds and destroyed all their work and killed the giants.}}
# {{lb|ang|transitive}} to [[owe]]
#* '''c. 990''', ''{{w|Wessex Gospels}}'', [https://archive.org/details/dahalgangodspelo00thor/page/n163/mode/2up Luke 16:5]
#*: {{quote|ang|''Þā þā gafolġieldan ġegaderode wǣron, þā sæġde hē þām forman, "Hū miċel '''sċealt''' þū mīnum hlāforde?"''|When the debtors had all been gathered together, he asked the first one, "How much do you '''owe''' my master?"}}
# {{lb|ang|auxiliary}} [[will]] (indicating the future)

